            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "1 car: 6 students; 2 cars: 8 students; 3 cars: 2 students.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p58-q29-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The middle observations are 1 and 2, so 6 students have one car. The total of 28 cars determines the remaining frequencies.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "There are 20 observations, so the median uses positions 10 and 11. Only four observations are zero; a median of $1.5$ therefore requires the middle values to be $1$ and $2$.\n\nExactly 10 students have at most one car. The number with one car is $10-4=6$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Total cars}&=20\\times1.4\\\\\\text{Total cars}&=28\\\\\\text{Remaining students}&=20-4-6\\\\\\text{Remaining students}&=10\\\\\\text{Remaining cars}&=28-6\\\\\\text{Remaining cars}&=22\\end{align*}$$\n\nTen two-car families would account for $10\\times2=20$ cars. The extra $22-20=2$ cars require two three-car families, leaving $10-2=8$ two-car families.\n\nThe missing frequencies are $6$, $8$ and $2$ students, respectively.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
